# Git and GitHub – Complete Steps

## 1. Open Project Folder
Open your project in VS Code and open the terminal.

```powershell
cd "C:\Users\Chandrashekhar\Downloads\backend\student-mangement-api"
```

## 2. Initialize Git
```powershell
git init
```
Creates a local Git repository.

## 3. Create .gitignore File

Create a file named `.gitignore` and add:

```text
node_modules/
.env
```

This prevents uploading node_modules and sensitive environment variables.

## 4. Check Git Status
```powershell
git status
```
Shows the files Git is tracking or detecting.

## 5. Add Files to Staging Area
```powershell
git add .
```
Adds all eligible project files to the staging area.

## 6. Commit Files
```powershell
git commit -m "Initial commit: Student Management API"
```
Saves the changes in the local Git repository.

## 7. Create GitHub Repository

1. Open https://github.com
2. Login to your account.
3. Click the + icon.
4. Select New repository.
5. Enter repository name: `student-management-api`.
6. Select Public or Private.
7. Click Create repository.

Create an empty repository without adding another README.

## 8. Connect Local Repository to GitHub
```powershell
git remote add origin https://github.com/Chandrashekhar1yh23/student-management-api.git
```
Connects the local project to the GitHub repository.

## 9. Verify Remote Connection
```powershell
git remote -v
```
Checks whether the GitHub repository is connected.

## 10. Check Branch Name
```powershell
git branch --show-current
```
Checks the current branch name. Mine is `main`.

## 11. Push Project to GitHub
```powershell
git push -u origin main
```
Uploads the committed project files to GitHub and sets the upstream branch.

## 12. Verify Upload

Open:
https://github.com/Chandrashekhar1yh23/student-management-api

Check whether all project files and README are visible. Make sure `.env` and `node_modules` are not uploaded.

